  view_file {
    absolute_path_uri: "file:///skills/system_skills/ultra_mode/references/games_3d.md"
    end_line: 133
    content: "# Game & 3D Surfaces, Procedural 3D Craft & Lossless Performance\n\nRead and apply these rules whenever the user request involves a Three.js / WebGL\nscene, 3D game, spatial simulation, or interactive 3D viewport. (If the request\nis a 2D or 3D game, also read\n[references/game_title_and_hud.md](references/game_title_and_hud.md).)\n\n## 1. Game & 3D Surfaces\n\nFor games and Three.js/WebGL scenes, texture the 1-3 surfaces where material\ndetail matters most (ground, road, primary masonry, hero props). Keep\narchitectural facades, beams, and props as clean 3D geometry rather than flat\nplanes with complex baked textures. Choose the source per surface and mix\nsources:\n\n-   **Shaders / procedural code**: Dynamic/moving surfaces (water, lava, fire,\n    force fields, clouds), graphic patterns (grids, neon lines, starfields),\n    toon/low-poly styles, large terrain, daytime skies\n    (`three/addons/objects/Sky.js`), and studio PBR reflections/lighting via\n    `three/addons/environments/RoomEnvironment.js` with\n    `THREE.PMREMGenerator(renderer)`. For realistic surface micro-relief (stone,\n    wood grain, brushed metal), generate a tileable grayscale `<canvas>`\n    `THREE.CanvasTexture` and bind it to `bumpMap` / `roughnessMap`.\n-   **`generate_image` (hosted local files)**: Realistic or stylized static\n    materials (brick, cobblestone, wood, rock - prompt for a flat orthographic\n    `1:1` seamless texture tile without perspective, lighting, or borders,\n    paired with calibrated `roughness`/`metalness` and a procedural\n    `CanvasTexture` `bumpMap`) and 2D art (signs, decals, card faces, `16:9`\n    painted backdrops). Never subdivide geometry for displacement. At most 3\n    material textures per scene. Opaque output only; no runtime Gemini calls.\n-   **All image textures & PBR safeguards**: Set `colorSpace =\n    THREE.SRGBColorSpace` on color maps only, `wrapS = wrapT =\n    THREE.RepeatWrapping`, and `anisotropy =\n    renderer.capabilities.getMaxAnisotropy()`. Whenever a texture `map` is bound\n    to `MeshStandardMaterial`, set `material.color` to `0xffffff` so it does not\n    darken the texture, and cap `metalness <= 0.25` when `scene.environment` is\n    not set so surfaces never reflect black vacuum.\n-   **Disjoint depth & contact shadows**: Offset stacked horizontal geometry\n    (floors, roads, rugs, decals) by `>= 0.02` on Y so coplanar faces never\n    Z-fight, and place a soft radial contact-shadow disc\n    (`THREE.CircleGeometry`, `opacity: 0.4`, `depthWrite: false`, `y = floorY +\n    0.015`) beneath actors, vehicles, and pickups for clear figure-ground\n    separation.\n-   **Zero-404 still holds**: Exact `generate_image` return paths are valid\n    local files (taking precedence over generic procedural-only defaults for\n    static masonry/wood/rock textures and 2D art). Everything else stays\n    procedural (`CanvasTexture`, shaders, `Sky.js`, `RoomEnvironment`). Never\n    fetch external texture or `.hdr` URLs.\n\n## 2. Procedural 3D Craft, Scene Layout, Lighting & Camera\n\n-   **Composite 3-12 part `THREE.Group` meshes (no single-primitive\n    placeholders)**: Never represent a vehicle, tree, structure, or hero prop\n    with a single default primitive (`BoxGeometry`, `SphereGeometry`,\n    `CylinderGeometry`, `CapsuleGeometry`). Build procedural 3D subjects as a\n    `THREE.Group` of **3-12 distinct parts** across three silhouette scales:\n    **(1) Primary silhouette** (hull, trunk, main body), **(2) Secondary read**\n    (wheels, canopy, turret, wings, roof), and **(3) Accent detail** (visor,\n    headlights, exhaust, trim), using `ExtrudeGeometry` (with bevels),\n    `LatheGeometry`, or `TubeGeometry` where appropriate. Vary `roughness` by\n    `>= 0.25` between adjacent parts (e.g., matte hull `0.75` vs. glossy canopy\n    `0.15` vs. metallic trim `0.3`) and apply subtle per-instance scale (`\302\26115%`)\n    and `setColorAt` tint variation on `InstancedMesh` foliage/rocks.\n-   **Outdoor vs. indoor perimeters, grounded props & spawn landmark vista**:\n    -   *Outdoor vs. indoor boundaries*: Indoor dungeons/temples use walls;\n        **outdoor arenas, villages, forests, and wastelands must never be boxed\n        inside a rectangular indoor wall floating in an empty void** \342\200\224 extend\n        terrain beyond the playable zone and bound outdoor perimeters with\n        natural barriers (cliffs, rock outcrops, dense tree groves, water/coast,\n        fences) and horizon fog fade-off (for floating sky islands, sculpt\n        tapered rock undersides over a sky/cloud backdrop).\n    -   *Grounding & spatial prop logic*: Pivot every prop and actor at bottom\n        center so it sits flush on `groundHeightAt(x, z)` (nothing floating);\n        anchor bookshelves, sconces, and portals flush against walls; cluster\n        crates/barrels in corners or against pillar bases; and frame every\n        doorway with solid wall jambs on both sides (never freestanding doors in\n        open floor).\n    -   *World compression & spawn vista*: Keep outdoor playable worlds compact\n        (`60-120m` radius, major landmarks every `20-35m` linked by curved\n        roads/trails, micro-vignette clusters every `8-12m` so walks never feel\n        empty), and frame at least 1 tall silhouette landmark in the initial\n        spawn camera view.\n-   **Tone mapping, warm/cool lighting contrast & fog-horizon continuity**:\n    Enable `renderer.toneMapping = THREE.ACESFilmicToneMapping`\n    (`toneMappingExposure = 1.0-1.2`). Light every 3D scene with deliberate\n    color-temperature contrast \342\200\224 pair a warm directional key light with a cool\n    sky/ground `THREE.HemisphereLight` fill (never flat white `AmbientLight`\n    alone), fit the directional light\'s orthographic shadow frustum tightly to\n    the active play area (`shadow.bias = -0.0005`), and match `scene.fog` (`Fog`\n    or `FogExp2`) to the horizon/sky background color so distant geometry blends\n    seamlessly.\n-   **Camera framing, exponential damping & wall pull-in**: Smooth all camera\n    follow positions and look targets with frame-rate-independent exponential\n    damping (`1 - Math.exp(-k * dt)`), raycast from the player/target anchor\n    toward the desired camera offset against environment meshes to pull the\n    camera in before clipping through walls, and apply decaying `trauma *\n    trauma` rotational shake or subtle FOV kick on heavy impacts and speed\n    boosts.\n\n## 3. Lossless Performance & Playability\n\n-   **Geometry, instancing & materials**: Create each `BufferGeometry` and\n    `Material` once and share across meshes; use `THREE.InstancedMesh` for\n    repeated props/tiles (`> 20` instances). Get surface detail from\n    `normalMap`/`bumpMap`/`roughnessMap` rather than high vertex counts (`1x1`\n    segments on flat surfaces, `16-32` on curves).\n-   **Stable WebGL lifecycle & zero-allocation render loop**: Mount the WebGL\n    `<canvas>` inside a dedicated empty `<div ref={canvasMountRef} />` outside\n    sibling React HUD overlays. Initialize `WebGLRenderer` and scene once\n    (`useEffect(..., [])`, cancelling the pending frame (`cancelAnimationFrame`\n    and `clearTimeout`) and calling `renderer.dispose()` on unmount), call\n    `renderer.compile(scene, camera)` once after setup to pre-warm shaders (skip\n    it when `isSoftwareGl`), and store callbacks/props in `useRef` so HUD state\n    updates never tear down the canvas or reset player position. Inside\n    `requestAnimationFrame`, pre-allocate scratch `Vector3`/`Matrix4` objects\n    outside the loop, raycast only against a small `interactables` array (never\n    `scene.children` recursively), and throttle React HUD `setState` updates to\n    `<= 10Hz` (or update DOM refs directly). Never use CSS `backdrop-filter` /\n    `backdrop-blur` on HUD elements over an active WebGL canvas (use solid or\n    `rgba(...)` fills so software WebGL rendering never stalls).\n-   **Lighting, shadow & software-WebGL budgets**: On GPUs, cap\n    `renderer.setPixelRatio(Math.min(window.devicePixelRatio, 2))`, limit\n    shadow-casting lights to 1 (tight shadow frustum, `1024x1024` map), and cap\n    active unshadowed `PointLight`s to 4-6 (distant lights use emissive\n    meshes/particles). When `isSoftwareGl`, follow the `threejs` skill\'s\n    software-WebGL path (pixel ratio `1`, skip shadows and multi-pass shaders,\n    keep the environment map and light intensities, render static scenes on\n    demand, and pace loops with `afterDraw()`).\n-   **Level geometry, spawn & controls**: Ensure dungeon/maze corridors are at\n    least 2 tiles wide with verified open paths connecting spawn, keys, and\n    exits without blocking walls. Spawn the player on an open path facing a\n    clear direction (never facing a wall), and listen for WASD/arrow keys on\n    `window`.\n"
    num_lines: 134
    num_bytes: 8739
    line_range_bytes: 8739
  }
}
