  planner_response {
    thinking: "**Analyzing the Physics Badge**\n\nI\'m currently delving into the Vercel Ship Interactive 3D Physics Lanyard Badge, specifically focusing on its implementation within `fattahmaulana/3D_CARD` and particularly `components/band/App.js`. My initial observation reveals a top anchor point, fixed at [0, 4, 0], and I\'m eager to understand how the physics engine drives the badge\'s interactive behavior. I am focusing on what makes it tick.\n\n\n**Examining the Lanyard Badge**\n\nI\'ve made progress in understanding the badge\'s structure. The 3D ID Card, connected via a rope joint chain, swings realistically. I\'m focusing on the implementation details, including the `CatmullRomCurve3` strap and the repeating texture. The metallic clip and clamp are also integral parts. I\'m now looking at the physics engine\'s influence on the swinging and pendulum effects. I\'m noting that this is implemented in React 19, and that there are peer dependency issues with the WASM, that should be resolved.\n\n\n**Rebuilding the Physics**\n\nI\'m now thinking about how to rebuild the physics simulation natively in Three.js, moving away from WebGL. My focus is on a deterministic Verlet/Rope-Constraint Physics Solver. This solver, in a decoupled unit-tested module, will handle gravity, distance constraints between the fixed point, joints, and the card, along with linear and angular damping. The goal is to make it super robust.\n\n\n**Detailing the Badge Components**\n\nNow, I\'m diving deeper into the specifics of each component. I\'m noting the high-resolution texture details of the lanyard, clamp, clip, and the 3D ID badge. I\'m focusing on the procedural `CanvasTexture` of the lanyard, the metallic properties of the clamp and clip, and the photo and monogram on the ID card. I\'m paying close attention to the textures, materials, and geometries used, such as `ExtrudeGeometry`, `CylinderGeometry` and the characteristics of the `MeshPhysicalMaterial`. I\'m going to look at how these interact with physics.\n\n\n"
    tool_calls {
