  planner_response {
    thinking: "**Investigating Image Clipping**\n\nI\'m currently focused on the image clipping issue observed in `/.playwright-cli/followup-lanyard-frameless.png`. The investigation centers around the camera settings within `Lanyard3DCard.tsx`. Specifically, the `PerspectiveCamera` with a 25-degree FOV and a z-position of 11.8 is under scrutiny. I\'m trying to determine if the camera\'s perspective or positioning is the root cause of the clipped left edge.\n\n\n**Calculating Frustum Dimensions**\n\nI\'ve calculated the camera\'s frustum dimensions based on the 25-degree FOV and z-position. At z=0, the visible width is 3.53 units and the height is 5.23. The half-width of the card is 1.075 units, so even a small horizontal swing causes clipping. The anchor point is also too high, causing the card\'s top to be near the canvas edge. I\'m exploring adjusting container width and camera parameters for a solution, using new dimensions and camera settings.\n\n\n**Revising Canvas Constraints**\n\nI\'ve determined the visible vertical and horizontal dimensions at z=0. The vertical half-height is 3.116, and the horizontal half-width is 2.84, considering an aspect ratio of 0.91. My focus is now on optimizing the anchor and rope length. I want the anchor point to be at the top edge, the strap to be visible, and the card positioned appropriately, ensuring enough clearance horizontally. I\'m calculating precise rope lengths to achieve a visually appealing and realistic card display.\n\n\n**Analyzing Swing Clearance**\n\nI\'ve calculated that with a card half-width of `0.975` and a horizontal half-frustum of `2.84`, the card can swing \302\2611.85 units without clipping the canvas. I\'m also considering the drop animation in the \'About\' section, noting the initial positions of the nodes and the performance impact on headless Chromium, where each frame takes about 85ms. This slower rendering affects the physics simulation, so I\'m investigating how many physics substeps per frame are needed for accurate timing.\n\n\n"
